<section class="section">
    <div class="container">
        <article class="legal">
            <p class="text-muted">Last updated: 2 November 2026</p>

            <p>This notice explains how Data Index, the operator of dataindex.online (the “site”), uses personal data. It is written for people in the United Kingdom and reflects the UK General Data Protection Regulation (UK GDPR) and the Data Protection Act 2018. Cookies are also covered by the Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations (PECR).</p>

            <h2>Who we are</h2>
            <p>Data Index is the controller for personal data described in this notice. The site publishes an index of UK company, VAT and postcode information drawn from public sources, so that people can look it up in one place.</p>

            <h2>How to contact us about your data</h2>
            <p>For any question, request or complaint about personal data, email <a href="mailto:info@dataindex.online">info@dataindex.online</a>. Please use the subject line “Data protection” so we can identify the message. We will respond within one month, or tell you if we need longer.</p>
            <p>We have not appointed a statutory data protection officer. That email address is the contact for data-protection matters.</p>

            <h2>Information published from public registers</h2>
            <p>Most of what the site shows is information about companies and places, taken from public sources:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Companies House:</strong> company name, number, status, type, registered office, industry codes, accounts dates and filing history. Filing history on a company page is shown from our stored copy when that copy was fetched within the last 24 hours, when Companies House cannot be reached, or when the visitor is a crawler Cloudflare has verified. Otherwise it is requested from the Companies House API for that visit. We show the document type, date and description. When you choose Run Financial Analysis, we use that filing history, together with filing history already stored for the company, to select up to three annual accounts. We then request those documents from Companies House and show figures taken from them. We do not keep a copy of the accounts documents. The figures are saved and shown on later visits until a confirmed visitor refreshes them. When you choose Find shareholders, we use incorporation, confirmation statement, annual return and share-capital filings to read how many shares are issued and who holds them. If those documents do not show the ownership, we request the people with significant control from Companies House. We store the number of shares issued and the resulting names and shareholdings, and replace them when a confirmed visitor refreshes them. We do not keep a copy of those documents.</li>
                <li><strong>HMRC:</strong> the business name, VAT number and address returned when someone searches for or validates a GB VAT number. We may keep that result so a later lookup can be answered, and we refresh it when HMRC returns a newer result.</li>
                <li><strong>Postcode data:</strong> location details for a postcode, together with companies and VAT-registered businesses linked to that postcode. A postcode page also shows residential sales from HM Land Registry Price Paid Data: the price, date, address and property type. It also shows a summary of street-level crime from data.police.uk for the latest published month, within a quarter of a mile of the postcode: how many crimes were recorded, by type, outcome and approximate street. Individual crime records are not stored. In Scotland that source only includes British Transport Police.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Information about a limited company is usually not personal data. It can be personal data where it identifies a living person, for example a sole trader’s name on a VAT record, or a home address that Companies House, HMRC or HM Land Registry publishes. Where that applies, we use the information to run this public index. The source is the relevant public body, or our stored copy of an earlier lookup. You can ask Companies House, HMRC or HM Land Registry to correct their records. You can also email us and ask us to review the copy we hold.</p>

            <h2>Information we collect when you use the site</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Search and lookup text.</strong> Words you type into company search, VAT search or VAT validation are sent to our server so we can return results. We do not build a profile of your searches.</li>
                <li><strong>Technical data.</strong> Our server records the usual details of a visit, such as IP address, browser type, the page requested, and the date and time. We use this to run and protect the site.</li>
                <li><strong>Email.</strong> If you write to info@dataindex.online, we keep your email address and the contents of the message so we can reply and, where relevant, handle a data-protection request.</li>
                <li><strong>Session cookie.</strong> The VAT validation form sets a session cookie so we can check that the form was submitted from our site.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The site does not offer user accounts, and it does not ask you to create a profile.</p>

            <h2>Why we use personal data</h2>
            <table class="data-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                        <th>Lawful basis under UK GDPR</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Publishing the company, VAT and postcode index</td>
                        <td>Legitimate interests: making public UK business information easier to find. Where a record identifies a person, that interest is limited to information the source already publishes for that purpose.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Answering searches and VAT checks</td>
                        <td>Legitimate interests: providing the lookup you asked for.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Keeping the site secure, including the session cookie on the VAT form and, when it is switched on, a Cloudflare visitor check before the index is shown</td>
                        <td>Legitimate interests: preventing misuse and confirming that form submissions come from the site.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Reading and replying to emails, including data-rights requests</td>
                        <td>Legitimate interests in corresponding with you. Where the law requires us to act on a request, legal obligation.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Understanding how the site is used, where analytics or session replay is switched on</td>
                        <td>Legitimate interests: keeping the site reliable and seeing which pages are used. You can refuse non-essential cookies in your browser.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <p>You can object to processing that is based on legitimate interests. We will stop unless we have a compelling reason to continue, or we need the data for a legal claim.</p>
            <p>We do not use personal data for automated decisions that produce legal or similarly significant effects. You do not create an account to read the site. When the visitor check is switched on, your browser completes that check with Cloudflare before the index is shown. Search and AI crawlers that Cloudflare has verified are allowed through. Those requests are answered from the stored index. The site does not call Companies House, HMRC, OpenAI, or data.police.uk for them, and a verified crawler cannot refresh a saved financial analysis or shareholding. A VAT check only works if you enter a VAT number.</p>

            <h2>Who we share information with</h2>
            <p>We share personal data with the organisations that help us run the site, and only for that purpose:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Companies House</strong> and <strong>HMRC</strong>, when the site requests a filing history, annual accounts for a financial analysis, or a VAT check. They are UK public bodies.</li>
                <li><strong>OpenAI</strong>, when Run Financial Analysis or Find shareholders is used and an OpenAI key is configured for the site. The text, page images, or PDF of up to three annual accounts, or of the share filings used to find shareholders, are sent so the figures or holders can be read. The documents are not stored by us for that purpose.</li>
                <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong>, when the visitor check is switched on. The check runs in your browser. We send Cloudflare the result token so it can tell us whether the check succeeded. We do not send Cloudflare the company pages you then open.</li>
                <li><strong>Our hosting provider</strong>, which stores the site and its logs.</li>
                <li><strong>Google</strong>, because the site loads fonts from Google. If Google Analytics is enabled, Google also receives usage data, including your IP address.</li>
                <li><strong>OpenStreetMap</strong>, when a postcode page shows an embedded map. Your browser connects directly to OpenStreetMap to load that map.</li>
                <li><strong>OpenReplay</strong>, if session replay is enabled. The recording script is loaded from OpenReplay. Recordings are sent to the ingest service configured for this site. Email addresses typed into a page are obscured in those recordings.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>We sell neither personal data nor company records as a contact list. We disclose information if the law requires it, for example a valid request from a law-enforcement body or the Information Commissioner.</p>

            <h2>Transfers outside the UK</h2>
            <p>Companies House and HMRC process the register lookups in the United Kingdom. Our own hosting is used to serve the site.</p>
            <p>Google may process font and analytics data in the United States. Where Google is certified, those transfers rely on the UK Extension to the EU-US Data Privacy Framework. Cloudflare may process the visitor check in the United States, and where Cloudflare is certified those transfers rely on the same framework. OpenAI may process the accounts pages and share filings sent for an analysis in the United States. OpenReplay’s script is loaded from its content network, which may be outside the UK. OpenStreetMap may handle the map request on infrastructure outside the UK. Where a transfer is not covered by UK adequacy regulations, we rely on the International Data Transfer Agreement or the Addendum to the EU standard contractual clauses.</p>

            <h2>How long we keep personal data</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Server logs:</strong> for up to 12 months, unless we need them for longer to investigate a security incident or deal with a legal claim.</li>
                <li><strong>Emails:</strong> until the enquiry is finished. We may keep a record for up to 6 years where the message relates to a legal right, a complaint, or a claim.</li>
                <li><strong>VAT lookup results:</strong> while we offer VAT search, and replaced when HMRC returns a newer result.</li>
                <li><strong>Company and postcode records:</strong> while the record remains part of the index.</li>
                <li><strong>Filing history:</strong> kept while the record remains part of the index. A company page shows that stored copy when it was fetched within the last 24 hours, when Companies House cannot be reached, or when the visitor is a crawler Cloudflare has verified. Otherwise it is requested from Companies House for that visit. A stored copy may also be used to choose annual accounts when you run a financial analysis.</li>
                <li><strong>Accounts documents:</strong> requested when a confirmed visitor runs or refreshes a financial analysis, read to produce the figures on screen, and not stored afterwards. The figures are saved and shown on the company page until a confirmed visitor refreshes them.</li>
                <li><strong>Shareholders:</strong> the number of shares issued, and the names, share classes and holdings read from the filings. When the filings do not show the ownership, the people with significant control are stored instead. Kept until a confirmed visitor refreshes them, then replaced. The documents are not stored.</li>
                <li><strong>Street-level crime:</strong> a summary for the latest published month within a quarter of a mile of a postcode. Requested from data.police.uk and replaced when it is more than 24 hours old. A verified crawler is shown the stored summary and does not trigger a new request. Individual crime records are not stored.</li>
                <li><strong>Session cookie:</strong> for the browser session.</li>
                <li><strong>Visitor check:</strong> stored only in a cookie on your device for 24 hours. We do not keep a separate record of the check.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Your rights</h2>
            <p>Where UK GDPR applies to information we hold about you, you can ask us to:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>tell you whether we hold your personal data, and for a copy of it;</li>
                <li>correct inaccurate personal data;</li>
                <li>erase personal data, in the circumstances the law allows;</li>
                <li>restrict how we use it;</li>
                <li>provide it to you or another controller, where the right to data portability applies;</li>
                <li>object to processing based on legitimate interests.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Email <a href="mailto:info@dataindex.online">info@dataindex.online</a>. We may ask for information that lets us find the right record and confirm we are dealing with you. Some company information is published because a public register publishes it. In that case we may direct you to Companies House or HMRC as the source, and we will still consider a request about the copy we hold.</p>

            <h2>Cookies and similar technologies</h2>
            <p>A cookie is a small file stored on your device. PECR treats a cookie that is strictly necessary to provide a service you asked for differently from optional analytics cookies.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Session cookie.</strong> Set when you use VAT validation, so we can check the form was sent from this site. It is strictly necessary for that form and lasts for the browser session.</li>
                <li><strong>Visitor check.</strong> When the check is switched on, a cookie named <code>di_visitor</code> records that this browser passed it. It is strictly necessary for access to the index, lasts for 24 hours, and is not used for advertising.</li>
                <li><strong>Google Analytics.</strong> Used only when analytics is switched on for the site. Google sets cookies such as <code>_ga</code> to recognise a browser across visits and produce aggregated statistics. You can block cookies in your browser, or use <a href="https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout" rel="noopener">Google’s Analytics opt-out</a>.</li>
                <li><strong>OpenReplay.</strong> Used only when session replay is switched on. It records how a page is used, such as navigation and clicks, so we can fix faults. It does not attach the recording to a user account.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Loading fonts from Google, and loading an OpenStreetMap embed, does not require a cookie from us. Your browser does send your IP address to those services when the page loads them.</p>

            <h2>Children</h2>
            <p>The site is a business-information service. It is not directed at children, and we do not knowingly collect personal data from children.</p>

            <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
            <p>We will post any change on this page and update the date at the top. A change that affects how we use personal data already collected will be described here before we rely on it.</p>

            <h2>How to complain</h2>
            <p>Please contact us first at <a href="mailto:info@dataindex.online">info@dataindex.online</a> so we can try to put things right. You can also complain to the Information Commissioner’s Office, the UK regulator for personal data:</p>
            <p>
                Information Commissioner’s Office<br>
